requirements/agent-protocols-config-removal.yaml:1:title: Removing configurability of `Jenkins.agentProtocols`
requirements/autocomplete-query-parameters.yaml:1:title: '[JENKINS-37241] Support for query parameters in autocomplete'
requirements/autocomplete-query-parameters.yaml:5:  A Jenkins administrator or plugin author exposing an autocomplete field may need extra query parameters to scope the results, but the current autocomplete widget only sends the search term and makes it impossible to express that richer filtering contract.
requirements/autocomplete-query-parameters.yaml:10:  Autocomplete endpoints should be able to declare additional query parameters through the descriptor contract, and the browser widget should include them when fetching suggestions. This should let server-side autocomplete logic honor contextual filters without changing the visible widget behavior for fields that do not need extra parameters.
requirements/autocomplete-query-parameters.yaml:15:  Autocomplete requests include any descriptor-defined extra query parameters alongside the typed term, existing fields without extra parameters keep working as before, and filtered suggestion endpoints can finally distinguish context that was previously unavailable to them.
requirements/autocomplete-suggestion-count.yaml:1:title: Allow plugins to customize number of suggestions in autocomplete
requirements/autocomplete-suggestion-count.yaml:6:  Some plugins need far more or far fewer autocomplete suggestions than Jenkins’s hard-coded default, but today they cannot express that preference in the rendered control.
requirements/autocomplete-suggestion-count.yaml:12:  Plugin authors should be able to set the maximum suggestion count per autocomplete field without forking the widget.
requirements/command-palette-header-guard.yaml:1:title: '`headerCommandPaletteButton` undefined when `JenkinsHeader` not loaded'
requirements/command-palette-header-guard.yaml:5:  A Jenkins page that does not render the standard `JenkinsHeader` should still be allowed to load frontend assets safely, but the Command Palette initializer currently assumes the header button exists and crashes on layouts that omit it.
requirements/command-palette-header-guard.yaml:10:  The Command Palette frontend should detect when `headerCommandPaletteButton` is absent and simply skip initialization on that page. Pages without `JenkinsHeader` should remain fully usable instead of failing because the palette tried to bind to a missing control.
requirements/command-palette-header-guard.yaml:15:  On layouts without the standard header, the page loads without JavaScript errors, the palette code exits early when `headerCommandPaletteButton` is missing, and pages that do include the header keep the normal Command Palette behavior.
requirements/command-palette-icons.yaml:1:title: Add icons to Command Palette
requirements/command-palette-icons.yaml:5:  A Jenkins user searching from the Command Palette needs enough visual context to distinguish jobs, users, views, agents, and other results at a glance, but the current results list is text-only and forces people to infer the target type from the name alone.
requirements/command-palette-icons.yaml:10:  Search results returned to the Command Palette should include both an `icon` and a `type` so the frontend can render the right symbol for each result class. Jobs should expose meaningful status-based icons, and the palette should present those icons consistently across the different result types Jenkins exports.
requirements/command-palette-icons.yaml:15:  Command Palette result payloads include `icon` and `type`, jobs surface a status-derived search icon, and users can visually distinguish result categories in the palette without opening each match first.
requirements/command-palette-ui-refinement.yaml:1:title: Refine Command Palette UI
requirements/command-palette-ui-refinement.yaml:5:  A Jenkins user opening the Command Palette expects it to feel responsive and polished, but the current dialog can show awkward loading flicker, abrupt closing behavior, and visual rough edges that make the interaction feel unfinished.
requirements/command-palette-ui-refinement.yaml:10:  The Command Palette should load results with smoother spinner timing, cleaner open-and-close animation, and more consistent spacing and visual treatment so it feels like a first-class navigation surface. The interface should preserve existing search behavior while improving the perceived responsiveness of searching and dismissing the palette.
requirements/dosaferestart-compatibility.yaml:1:title: '[JENKINS-73838] Compatibility for `Jenkins#doSafeRestart(StaplerRequest, String)`'
requirements/dosaferestart-compatibility.yaml:6:  Older callers still invoke `Jenkins#doSafeRestart(StaplerRequest, String)`, and the newer request-type migration should not break those integrations abruptly.
requirements/dosaferestart-compatibility.yaml:18:  `Jenkins#doSafeRestart(StaplerRequest, String)` remains available as a deprecated bridge and delegates correctly to the newer safe-restart implementation.
requirements/dosaferestart-lts-backport.yaml:5:  Plugin or integration code that still calls the deprecated `doSafeRestart` overloads may pass a null request object in compatibility scenarios, and Jenkins currently throws before the restart flow can be delegated to the newer implementation.
requirements/dosaferestart-lts-backport.yaml:10:  The deprecated `doSafeRestart(StaplerRequest)` and `doSafeRestart(StaplerRequest, String)` entry points should remain tolerant of `null` and forward that call through to the modern restart path without crashing. This is a compatibility fix for callers that still rely on those overloads in the 2.479.1 LTS line.
requirements/dosaferestart-lts-backport.yaml:15:  Calling either deprecated `doSafeRestart` overload with a null request no longer throws during request conversion, the restart path still reaches the current implementation, and existing LTS callers do not need to special-case null before invoking Jenkins.
requirements/dosaferestart-null-compatibility.yaml:1:title: Allow for null to be passed to `doSafeRestart`
requirements/dosaferestart-null-compatibility.yaml:5:  Plugin or integration code that still calls the deprecated `doSafeRestart` overloads may pass a null request object in compatibility scenarios, and Jenkins currently throws before the restart flow can be delegated to the newer implementation.
requirements/dosaferestart-null-compatibility.yaml:10:  The deprecated `doSafeRestart(StaplerRequest)` and `doSafeRestart(StaplerRequest, String)` entry points should remain tolerant of `null` and forward that call through to the modern restart path without crashing. This is a compatibility fix for callers that still rely on those overloads.
requirements/dosaferestart-null-compatibility.yaml:15:  Calling either deprecated `doSafeRestart` overload with a null request no longer throws during request conversion, the restart path still reaches the current implementation, and existing callers do not need to special-case null before invoking Jenkins.
requirements/keyboard-scroll-selection.yaml:5:  A Jenkins user navigating Command Palette results with the keyboard expects arrow-key movement to keep the selected item visible, but the current scroll helper can miss cases where the selected element is undefined and keyboard navigation becomes unreliable.
requirements/keyboard-scroll-selection.yaml:10:  Keyboard-driven navigation should tolerate the absence of a selected item and only try to scroll when a real selection exists. The Command Palette should continue to wire keyboard handling only while it is open so arrow-key navigation stays smooth instead of tripping over an uninitialized selection.
requirements/keyboard-scroll-selection.yaml:15:  Using the keyboard to move through Command Palette results no longer throws or stalls when nothing is selected yet, scrolling resumes once an item is actually selected, and navigation remains scoped to the palette while it is open.
requirements/typedfilter-race-leak.yaml:1:title: Race condition & memory leak in `TypedFilter`
requirements/typedfilter-race-leak.yaml:5:  A Jenkins administrator hitting rare startup or page-render races should not see Stapler type filtering fail unpredictably or retain class metadata indefinitely, but the current `TypedFilter` cache can both leak memory and behave unsafely under concurrent access.
requirements/typedfilter-race-leak.yaml:10:  `TypedFilter` should compute Stapler relevance in a thread-safe, class-scoped way so concurrent requests do not race on shared cache state and class-loader churn does not leave stale entries behind. The filtering behavior itself should stay the same for acceptable and unacceptable types; the change is about making that decision reliable and leak-resistant.
requirements/typedfilter-race-leak.yaml:15:  Concurrent request processing no longer depends on a mutable global map inside `TypedFilter`, class relevance checks remain consistent under load, and long-running Jenkins instances do not accumulate stale cache entries from that filter implementation.
