requirements/atomicfilewriter-temp-names.yaml:1:title: Simplify AtomicFileWriter and use clearer temporary file names
requirements/atomicfilewriter-temp-names.yaml:10:  `AtomicFileWriter` should create clearer temporary file names derived from the destination path and should remember after the first `AtomicMoveNotSupportedException` that the platform requires a non-atomic fallback. Once that fallback is known, Jenkins should replace the target cleanly without repeating the unsupported atomic attempt on every write.
requirements/os-eol-warning-date.yaml:1:title: '[JENKINS-73845] Fix OperatingSystemEndOfLifeAdminMonitor endOfLifeDate displayed on first warning day'
requirements/os-eol-warning-date.yaml:10:  `OperatingSystemEndOfLifeAdminMonitor` should activate on the first warning day and expose `getEndOfLifeDate()` as the actual configured end-of-life date for the matched operating system, not a placeholder or later-updated value. Administrators should be able to trust the date shown in the warning as soon as the banner first appears.
requirements/peepholepermalink-cache-extension.yaml:1:title: '`PeepholePermalink.Cache`'
requirements/peepholepermalink-cache-extension.yaml:5:  Plugin developers who customize permalink lookup need a supported way to replace the cache behind `PeepholePermalink`, and Jenkins operators expect nearest-build navigation to keep returning the correct build around gaps created by deleted runs. The current implementation hard-codes the cache and leaves some lookup semantics implicit.
requirements/peepholepermalink-cache-extension.yaml:10:  `PeepholePermalink` should expose its cache as an extension point through `PeepholePermalink.Cache`, with persisted entries using the existing `id + " " + (build == null ? -1 : build.getNumber())` storage shape so alternate cache implementations remain compatible. At the same time, nearest-build and nearest-old-build resolution should preserve the exact numeric behavior Jenkins expects when build numbers are missing.
